Chlorophyll Riot

Creative Catalyst: Botanists wired electrodes to high-altitude rainforest ferns to trigger jagged, euphoric soundscapes from their natural bio-electrical bursts.

This genre is a violent garden. It ditches the "raw power" of punk for "organic growth," replacing aggressive power chords with hyper-speed glissandos performed on modified synthesizers that react to soil acidity levels. The mood is manic and jolly, like a carnivorous plant laughing at a drought. The PAD MM Dark Sleep drone provides a thick, velvety loam from which the frantic, treble-heavy melody erupts. It is celebratory, abrasive, and dangerously alive.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: 170–190 BPM, frenetic and skittering, mimicking the jittery firing of nerve cells in flora.
*   Harmonic Palette: Pentatonic scales disrupted by microtonal "leaf-tears" (random frequency pitch-bends).
*   Production Signatures: 
    1. Rhizome-Mapping: Running drum tracks through a feedback loop controlled by real-time humidity sensors in a terrarium. 
    2. Sap-Gating: Using a side-chain compressor triggered exclusively by the electrical voltage of a blooming orchid. 
    3. Bio-Drenching: Capturing the sound of plants vibrating under UV light to create a natural, crackling "white noise" floor.
*   Dynamics: Start with a slow, creeping cinematic drone that abruptly fractures into an explosive, high-energy sprint without a crescendo.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born in the high-canopy research stations of the Ecuadorian Andes, the Chlorophyll Riot scene began when three renegade botanists hacked their sensor rigs to broadcast via pirate radio. The subculture—known as "The Green Noise"—wears bioluminescent moss embedded into translucent PVC raincoats, gathering in greenhouses to dance in a state of hyper-oxygenated, frantic euphoria. Their rituals involve "pollinating" the audience with glitter-dusted mist during the most intense synth-swell climaxes. It is a fashion of transparent utility meets swamp-punk decay, where hair is dyed in shades of bruised fern and rusted earth.

Typical Instruments: Modified Moog Sub 37 (retuned to match plant bio-frequencies), high-velocity electric guitars with "soil-dampened" strings, and the PAD MM Dark Sleep drone as a constant, heavy atmospheric bed.

For Fans Of: Death Grips, Brian Eno’s ambient work, The Slits, Midori Takada.

Fictional Band Name: Stomata Thrash

Band Description: A trio of former Oxford biologists who use contact microphones attached to giant Monstera leaves to create percussive hits. They play with feral intensity, wearing clear plastic lab coats stained with chlorophyll and dirt, frequently swapping guitar cables for vines mid-set to shift the tonal "ph" of the room.

